# CSET Strict Engineering Standards

1. ZERO EMOJI POLICY (CRITICAL)
   - NEVER use emojis in code, markup, buttons, tabs, empty states, or UI components under any circumstances.
   - Use custom vector SVG icons or clean minimalist typography for all visual indicators and controls.

2. 200-LINE FILE LIMIT
   - Keep individual code files strictly under 200 lines for optimal context management.
   - Split large components into focused feature modules.
   - Extract utility functions and database queries into dedicated service classes.

3. ARCHITECTURE & DIRECTORY STRUCTURE
   - Follow Feature-Driven Domain Architecture: `src/lib/features/<domain>/`
   - Domains contain isolated `components/`, `server/`, and optional `schema.ts`.
   - Shared platform code lives in `src/lib/core/` (db, auth, policy, i18n).

4. API SECURITY & ACCESS CONTROL
   - Enforce server-side role validation on every action: Core Team vs Squad Lead vs Member.
   - Prevent IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) by checking tenant/squad ownership directly in D1 queries.
   - Always validate input schemas using Zod before passing to database layers.

5. DESIGN SYSTEM FIDELITY
   - Strictly use `cset-ui` components (Button, Card, InputField, Select, Dialog, Sheet, toast, Badge).
   - Use Blueprint Schematic styling: technical corner-ticks, monospaced eyebrow badges (`eyebrow-badge`), and responsive layouts.
